<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the model’s APA performance, we used Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC) to measure the correlation between human and model-predicted pronunciation scores. For MDD evaluation, we assessed the model’s speech recognition performance using Word Error Rate (WER). Additionally, for phoneme recognition results, we calculated Phoneme Error Rate (PER) and F1-score by comparing the model outputs with reference phoneme transcriptions to measure the accuracy of mispronunciation detection.</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{WER}=\frac{I+D+S}{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>WER</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{WER}=\frac{I+D+S}{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PER}=\frac{I+D+S}{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PER</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PER}=\frac{I+D+S}{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The WER metric quantifies the performance of speech recognition models. In the WER formula (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.3. Evaluation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ English Pronunciation Evaluation without Complex Joint Training: LoRA Fine-tuned Speech Multimodal LLM" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I</span> represents the number of insertion errors, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> represents the number of deletion errors, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">S</span> represents the number of substitution errors, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N</span> represents the total number of words in the reference. The sum of these errors is divided by the total number of words to obtain the error rate. Similarly, PER (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.3. Evaluation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ English Pronunciation Evaluation without Complex Joint Training: LoRA Fine-tuned Speech Multimodal LLM" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) was calculated using the same approach as WER, but at the phoneme level rather than the word level.</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Precision}=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Precision</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Precision}=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Recall}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Recall</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Recall}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{F1 score}=2\times\frac{\text{precision}\times\text{recall}}{\text{precision}+\text{recall}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>F1 score</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>precision</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>recall</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>precision</mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>recall</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{F1 score}=2\times\frac{\text{precision}\times\text{recall}}{\text{precision}+\text{recall}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In the precision and recall formulas (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.3. Evaluation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ English Pronunciation Evaluation without Complex Joint Training: LoRA Fine-tuned Speech Multimodal LLM" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.3. Evaluation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ English Pronunciation Evaluation without Complex Joint Training: LoRA Fine-tuned Speech Multimodal LLM" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), <span id="S4.SS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TP</span> represents True Positive (correctly identified mispronunciations), <span id="S4.SS3.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FP</span> represents False Positive (incorrectly flagged correct pronunciations), and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FN</span> represents False Negative (missed mispronunciations). These metrics were then used to compute the F1-score (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.3. Evaluation ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ English Pronunciation Evaluation without Complex Joint Training: LoRA Fine-tuned Speech Multimodal LLM" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), which provides a balanced measure of the model’s mispronunciation detection capability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2. Model Configuration ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ English Pronunciation Evaluation without Complex Joint Training: LoRA Fine-tuned Speech Multimodal LLM" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the APA and MDD task training results. Both fine-tuning strategies significantly improved over the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no training</span> baseline, which showed high initial error rates (WER 0.97, PER 0.792) and low detection performance (F1-score 0.143). The LoRA approach consistently yielded superior results compared to the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unfreeze</span> strategy across all metrics. The <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LoRA</span> approach achieved its lowest Word Error Rate (WER) of 0.139 at epoch 2 and its best Phoneme Error Rate (PER) of 0.114, F1-score (0.724), precision (0.728), and recall (0.721) at epoch 3. While the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unfreeze</span> strategy showed performance gains up to epoch 4, its best scores (WER 0.142, PER 0.142, F1-score 0.667) remained less optimal than those achieved with the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LoRA</span> approach. These findings indicate that LoRA fine-tuning, particularly around epochs 2 and 3, was the more effective strategy for the MDD task rather than full audio layer fine-tuning in this experiment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
